<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A fintech offering accounts, cards and small loans had a mature rules engine and a transaction-level risk model. Both looked at one event or one account at a time. The losses that hurt most came from coordinated activity: clusters of new accounts opened from the same few devices, with addresses written slightly differently, paying out to a shared set of payees. Each account looked unremarkable alone. The pattern only appeared when someone noticed the connections by hand, usually after the money had moved.</p>
<p>The question was how to make those connections visible automatically, fast enough to matter, without replacing the decisioning stack that already worked.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Resolve before you connect</strong>: one entity per real person, device, address and payee.</li>
<li><strong>Explainable links</strong>: every edge in a ring traceable to the evidence behind it.</li>
<li><strong>Features, not a new decision engine</strong>: graph signals feed existing models and rules.</li>
<li><strong>Fresh enough for onboarding and payouts</strong>, the two moments where stopping fraud pays.</li>
<li><strong>Investigators in the loop</strong>, able to confirm, split or merge entities.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Events</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Onboarding<em>identity, device</em></span><span>Logins<em>device, network</em></span><span>Payments<em>payer, payee</em></span><span>Case outcomes<em>confirmed fraud</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Stream</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Kafka topics<em>keyed by entity</em></span><span>Normalisation<em>addresses, names, devices</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Resolve</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Blocking<em>candidate pairs</em></span><span class="hl">Probabilistic matching<em>scored, explainable</em></span><span>Analyst overrides<em>merge, split</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Graph</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Entity graph<em>people, devices, addresses, payees</em></span><span>Connected components<em>ring candidates</em></span><span>Graph features<em>size, proximity to fraud</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Use</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Feature store<em>online lookups</em></span><span>Risk scoring<em>existing models</em></span><span>Investigator UI<em>ring view with evidence</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Layered architecture: events are resolved into entities, connected in a graph, and returned to existing scoring as features.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Confirmed fraud outcomes from closed cases were fed back as events too, so proximity to known fraud stayed current and the risk models had better labels over time.</p>
<p>The graph model was kept deliberately small, a handful of entity and relationship types with clear definitions, following the approach in <a href="/insights/ontology-vs-knowledge-graph-vs-semantic-layer/">ontology vs knowledge graph vs semantic layer</a>.</p>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Stream</b><span>Onboarding, login and payment events published to Kafka, keyed by source identifiers</span></li>
<li><b>Normalise</b><span>Standardise addresses, names, phone numbers and device fingerprints</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Resolve</b><span>Block into candidate pairs, score matches, assign stable entity IDs with match evidence</span></li>
<li><b>Connect</b><span>Upsert entities and relationships into the graph incrementally</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Featurise</b><span>Compute component size, shared-attribute counts and distance to confirmed fraud</span></li>
<li><b>Act</b><span>Features served to risk scoring; high-risk rings opened as investigator cases</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Entity resolution sits before the graph, so the graph only ever connects resolved entities.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Current entity state was held in compacted topics, where Kafka retains the latest value for each key, so downstream services could rebuild their view without replaying full history<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Streaming patterns are in <a href="/insights/change-data-capture-event-streams/">change data capture and event streams</a>.</p>

<h2>The hard part: entity resolution you can defend</h2>
<p>Matching everything to everything is quadratic, so resolution started with blocking: cheap keys that limit comparisons to plausible candidates, a well-studied family of techniques<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Matching then used a probabilistic, Fellegi-Sunter style model of the kind implemented in Splink, which scores each comparison field and can be trained without labelled pairs<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. What made it defensible:</p>
<ul>
<li>Every merge stored the field-level evidence and score, so an investigator could see why two records became one entity.</li>
<li>Thresholds were set separately for automatic merges and for "possible match" links that investigators saw but models treated with caution.</li>
<li>Shared infrastructure entities (a popular office address, a carrier-grade NAT IP) were flagged so they did not collapse unrelated customers into giant false rings.</li>
<li>Analyst merges and splits were written back as overrides that survived reprocessing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ring candidates came from connected components over the resolved graph, which groups nodes reachable from one another regardless of edge direction<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. More detail in <a href="/insights/entity-resolution-knowledge-graphs/">entity resolution for knowledge graphs</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>In fraud work a false merge is not a modelling error, it is an accusation. Explainable matches and reversible overrides are what make a graph usable by investigators.</p></div>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Platform</small><ol><li>Resolves entities</li><li>Updates graph</li><li class="hl">Serves ring features</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Risk scoring</small><ol><li>Reads features</li><li>Scores event</li><li>Opens case on high risk</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Investigator</small><ol><li>Reviews ring and evidence</li><li class="hl">Confirms, splits or merges</li><li>Records outcome</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Investigator outcomes flow back as labels and overrides.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Model</b><ul><li>Entity and relationship types</li><li>Known fraud cases mapped</li><li>Data sources inventoried</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Batch proof</b><ul><li>Historical resolution</li><li>Rings found in past cases</li><li>Investigator feedback</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Streaming</b><ul><li>Incremental resolution</li><li>Online features</li><li>Model integration</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Operate</b><ul><li>Investigator UI</li><li>Override workflow</li><li>Runbooks and hand-off</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>A batch proof on historical cases before any real-time work.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Probabilistic, explainable matching</td><td>Exact keys only</td><td>Catches variant spellings while keeping evidence for every merge</td></tr>
<tr><td>Graph features into existing models</td><td>Separate graph-based decision engine</td><td>Adds signal without replacing a working decision stack</td></tr>
<tr><td>Small, defined graph model</td><td>Load every attribute as a node</td><td>Fewer false connections and simpler queries</td></tr>
<tr><td>Batch proof before streaming</td><td>Build real-time first</td><td>Validates value on known cases before paying for latency</td></tr>
<tr><td>Durable analyst overrides</td><td>Recompute from scratch each run</td><td>Human corrections are not lost on reprocessing</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: start with historical confirmed cases and show investigators the rings the graph would have surfaced. Differently: build the shared-infrastructure entity list on day one. It is the main source of false rings, and it is easier to design for than to clean up.</p>
<p>If you are considering a knowledge graph for risk or investigations, the <a href="/ontology-knowledge-graph/">ontology and knowledge graph</a> page describes how I run these engagements, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
